/-!
# The calculus of restriction morphisms on the small crystalline site

Fix a global crystalline situation `(p, (S, I, γ), X)`.  The covering families of the small
crystalline site `Cris(X/S)` are generated by the families of restrictions `A|_V ⟶ A` of an object
to an open cover of its ambient scheme, `Crystalline.GlobalCrystallineSituation.restrictCoverage`.
This file assembles the calculus those morphisms obey: restriction to the whole ambient scheme is
invertible, a restriction of a restriction is a restriction, an arbitrary morphism pulls a
restriction back to a restriction, and consequently the iterated fibre products of a family of
restrictions over the object exist and are again restrictions, to the intersection of the opens.

That last statement is the input the Čech machinery takes: the Čech complex of [Stacks, Tag 03AM]
and the criterion of [Stacks, Tag 03F9] ask for the finite products of a covering family inside
the slice category `Over A`, and the identification of such a product with the restriction to an
intersection of opens is what lets affineness of the opens be propagated to the product, through
`AlgebraicGeometry.IsAffineOpen.iInf`.

## Main results

Everything below lives in the namespace
`Crystalline.GlobalCrystallineSituation.SmallSite.CrystallineThickening`.

* `isIso_restrictOpenHom_top`, `isIso_restrictOpenHom_of_eq_top` and `isoRestrictOpenTop` — the
  restriction of an object of `Cris(X/S)` to the whole of its ambient scheme is that object.
* `isIso_restrictOpenMap_restrictOpenHom`, `restrictOpenMap_restrictOpenLE` and
  `isoRestrictOpenRestrictOpen` — for opens `V ≤ W` of the ambient scheme of `A`, the restriction
  of `A|_W` to the preimage of `V` is `A|_V`, compatibly with `restrictOpenLE`.
* `isPullback_restrictOpenMap` and `hasPullback_restrictOpenHom` — a restriction pulls back along
  an arbitrary morphism `g : B ⟶ A` of `Cris(X/S)` to the restriction of `B` to the preimage of
  the open, so the fibre product of a restriction with an arbitrary morphism exists.
* `restrictOpenFan` and `isLimit_restrictOpenFan` — the restriction of `A` to the intersection of
  a finite family of opens of its ambient scheme is the product, in `Over A`, of the restrictions
  to the members of the family; `hasProduct_restrictOpenHom` is the existence statement and
  `isoRestrictOpenIInf` the identification of any such product with that restriction.
* `hasProduct_restrictOpenCover` — the same existence statement in the shape the Čech machinery
  takes it, for the covering family `restrictOpenCover`.
* `isAffine_restrictOpen` — the ambient scheme of a restriction to an affine open is affine.

## Implementation notes

The ambient scheme of `A|_V` is the open subscheme `V.toScheme` **by `rfl`**: each of
`SmallSite.CrystallineThickening.restrictOpen`,
`Crystalline.GlobalCrystallineSituation.Thickening.restrictOpen`, `Crystalline.SchemeThickening`'s
`restrict` and `Crystalline.DividedPowerScheme`'s `restrict` is a structure literal whose relevant
field is the next one down, ending at `scheme := V.toScheme`.  That is why `isAffine_restrictOpen`
is literally its own hypothesis, and why
`AlgebraicGeometry.IsAffineOpen.iInf` applies to the product of a family of restrictions with no
transport: the identification `isLimit_restrictOpenFan` already names the product
`A.restrictOpen (⨅ a, V a)`, whose ambient scheme is `(⨅ a, V a).toScheme` on the nose.

`CategoryTheory.Limits.HasPullbacks` for the ambient category of divided power thickenings is a
known gap — see the discussion in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Crystalline.SelfProductDiagonal` — and it is not needed here.  This
file routes around it by giving the pullbacks of *restriction* morphisms directly: every square
exhibited below is proved from the universal property
`SmallSite.CrystallineThickening.exists_comp_restrictOpenHom_eq` of a restriction together with the
fact that its structure morphism is a monomorphism,
`SmallSite.CrystallineThickening.mono_restrictOpenHom`.  Those two facts are all the pullbacks that
the Čech arguments on the site require, which is exactly the point of
`Stacks.Sites.PreservesCoveringPullbacks`: it asks for pullbacks of covering arrows only.

`isLimit_restrictOpenFan` is proved directly rather than by iterating the binary case through
`Stacks.Sites.hasProduct_over_fin`, because the direct argument simultaneously *identifies* the
product — the lifting property applies verbatim to an arbitrary finite family, since a morphism
whose ambient image lies in every member of the family lies in their intersection.  It is also
stronger in two ways: the index type is arbitrary (not `Fin (n + 1)`), and the empty family is
allowed, where the intersection is `⊤` and the statement reduces to `isIso_restrictOpenHom_top`.
`hasProduct_restrictOpenCover` records that the route through
`Stacks.Sites.hasProduct_fibreProducts` from `hasPullback_restrictOpenHom` reaches the same
conclusion, in the spelling `Stacks.Sites.exists_preZeroHypercover_le` produces.

